    32	    torn back down, 2026-08-14 — see git history). But "one endpoint with BOTH
    33	    Xcode tools available, prefixed so there's no confusion" is a real,
    34	    different thing worth having alongside the single-upstream daemon, not
    35	    instead of it. Both modes share every line of connection/click/reconnect
    36	    logic below via the Upstream class — no duplicated logic between them.
    37	
    38	This is a dumb passthrough, not a real aggregator: it forwards list_tools/call_tool
    39	to the relevant upstream ClientSession(s) and returns whatever comes back. It does
    40	not interpret tool semantics. Calls to a given upstream are serialized through
    41	that upstream's own lock (tolerance for concurrent overlapping calls hasn't been
    42	tested, so this starts correctness-first — tool calls are already human-paced, so
    43	serialization shouldn't be felt in practice); different upstreams' calls are
    44	fully independent of each other.
    45	
    46	VALIDATED 2026-08-14 (all live, against a real Xcode):
    47	- One connection tolerates many sequential calls of different shapes, no reconnect
    48	  needed (spike.py, then a real multi-call session through this daemon).
    49	- Quitting Xcode does NOT crash this process — it keeps serving HTTP, but the
    50	  mcpbridge child dies and every call after that fails clean with "Connection
    51	  closed".
    52	- The approval prompt's PID (confirmed from the actual dialog, not assumed) is
    53	  THIS daemon's own PID, not the ephemeral mcpbridge child's — so as long as this
    54	  process keeps running, reconnecting mcpbridge in a loop should NOT need a fresh
    55	  approval each time, only the very first connection (or after a genuine
    56	  permission reset) does.
    57	- The approval prompt is requested LAZILY, on the first list_tools call, not at
    58	  the initial connect — the per-upstream reconnect loop polls for it (and
    59	  clicks it) continuously in the connected steady-state too, not just while
    60	  reconnecting.
    61	- Xcode's approval dialogs do NOT stack — only one shows at a time, and a stale
    62	  unanswered one blocks the next (including this daemon's own) from appearing.
    63	  The click logic reads each dialog's own PID: clicks Allow for our own live
    64	  pid, clicks Don't Allow for a dead pid (nobody's waiting on it — a plain
    65	  os.kill(pid,0) liveness check, no PID-history file needed), leaves any other
    66	  live pid's dialog strictly alone.
    67	- Drew's xcode-mcp (drews-xcode-mcp) has NO approval-dialog behavior at all —
    68	  folder-allowlist auth (XCODEMCP_ALLOWED_FOLDERS / --allowed) instead of a
    69	  live per-PID popup. Its Upstream instance just never finds a dialog to click;
    70	  harmless no-op, no special-casing needed.
    71	
    72	Env overrides — SINGLE-upstream mode (default):
    73	  XCODE_MCP_FRONT_UPSTREAM_CMD    default "xcrun"
    74	  XCODE_MCP_FRONT_UPSTREAM_ARGS   default "mcpbridge" (space-separated)
    75	  XCODE_MCP_FRONT_REQUIRE_XCODE   default "1" — wait for Xcode.app before connecting.
    76	                                   Set "0" for an upstream (like drews-xcode-mcp)
    77	                                   that doesn't need a live Xcode process at all.
    78	  XCODE_MCP_FRONT_AUTO_ALLOW      default "1" — click-Allow-automatically. Off
    79	                                   switch for anyone who'd rather approve by hand.
    80	  XCODE_MCP_FRONT_SERVER_NAME     default "xcode-mcp-front"
    81	
    82	Env overrides — MULTI-upstream mode (mutually exclusive with the single-upstream
    83	UPSTREAM_CMD/ARGS/REQUIRE_XCODE vars above — set THIS instead):
    84	  XCODE_MCP_FRONT_UPSTREAMS       semicolon-separated upstream specs, each
    85	                                   "name:require_xcode:command:arg1,arg2,...".
    86	                                   Example:
    87	                                   "xcode:1:xcrun:mcpbridge;drews:0:uvx:drews-xcode-mcp"
    88	                                   Tool names are prefixed "<name>__" in the
    89	                                   merged tool list and un-prefixed again when
    90	                                   routing a call back to that upstream.
    91	
    92	Env overrides — both modes:
    93	  XCODE_MCP_FRONT_HOST             default "127.0.0.1" (do not bind wider — no auth layer)
    94	  XCODE_MCP_FRONT_PORT             default "8765"
    95	  XCODE_MCP_FRONT_XCODE_APP_PATH   default "/Applications/Xcode.app" — the reconnect
    96	                                    loop matches THIS exact binary path, not a bare
    97	                                    process name (Xcode.app and Xcode-beta.app share
    98	                                    both their process name and bundle ID, so only
    99	                                    the full path tells them apart — confirmed live)
   100	  XCODE_MCP_FRONT_RECONNECT_POLL_S default "5" (seconds between reconnect attempts)
   101	  XCODE_MCP_FRONT_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_S default "15" (per-attempt connect timeout, so a
   102	                                    stuck approval dialog can't hang a whole attempt
   103	                                    forever — it just times out and the next tick
   104	                                    tries the click again)
