   256	
   257	# THE CLICKER IS LOAD-BEARING AND MUST NOT BE "DESIGNED AWAY". Xcode's approval is keyed to
   258	# the CONNECTING PID, and a daemon restart always mints a new one, so no grant survives a
   259	# bounce — not a signed app bundle, not the "don't ask again for this agent binary" checkbox,
   260	# which only lasts until Xcode itself restarts. An unattended daemon therefore has to answer
   261	# its own prompt or it can never connect after any restart. Jonathan, 2026-08-30, correcting
   262	# a suggestion to remove it: "the daemons HAVE to depend on a clicker."
   263	#
   264	# That is why a defect in this function is not cosmetic. It is the only path to a working
   265	# connection, so a branch that silently declines to click takes the whole daemon down while
   266	# every log line still says "connected".
   267	
   268	
   269	# How long another process's approval dialog is left alone before we clear it. Only once we
   270	# have been blocked for this long is it fair to assume nobody is coming to answer it.
   271	# HOW LONG A FOREIGN DIALOG MAY BLOCK US BEFORE WE CLEAR IT. This MUST be comfortably less
   272	# than CONNECT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS or two daemons deadlock by arithmetic rather than by bad luck.
   273	#
   274	# It was 45 against a 15-second connect timeout. Xcode's approval dialogs do not stack, so when
   275	# both front daemons are up, each one's clicker finds the other's dialog, correctly classifies it
   276	# as foreign-and-alive, and waits out a grace period three times longer than the connection
   277	# attempt it is holding up. Its own prompt therefore never surfaces, its list_tools times out,
   278	# it tears the child down — which withdraws its request — and both sides repeat forever. That is
   279	# the "do two bridges interfere" question Jonathan asked: they do, and this constant is why.
   280	#
   281	# Clearing a live foreign dialog is not destructive. That process re-prompts on its next attempt,
   282	# which for these daemons is RECONNECT_POLL_SECONDS away, so two of them alternate and both get
   283	# through instead of neither.
   284	FOREIGN_DIALOG_GRACE_SECONDS = float(os.environ.get("XCODE_MCP_FRONT_FOREIGN_GRACE", "6"))
   285	
   286	# The relationship above is load-bearing, so it is checked rather than trusted to a comment. A
   287	# grace period at or beyond the connect timeout is a silent, permanent deadlock whenever a second
   288	# client exists, and it presents as "mcpbridge refuses to serve tools" — which is exactly how it
   289	# presented, for a night.
   290	if FOREIGN_DIALOG_GRACE_SECONDS >= CONNECT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS:
   291	    raise SystemExit(
   292	        "xcode-mcp-front: XCODE_MCP_FRONT_FOREIGN_GRACE (%.1fs) must be less than "
   293	        "XCODE_MCP_FRONT_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_S (%.1fs). A foreign approval dialog that outlives the "
   294	        "connection attempt it is blocking can never be cleared in time, so two daemons deadlock "
   295	        "and every symptom looks like Xcode refusing to serve tools."
   296	        % (FOREIGN_DIALOG_GRACE_SECONDS, CONNECT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS))
   297	
   298	# How long an upstream may make NO progress before we let launchd restart us. See
   299	# stall_watchdog(). Generous on purpose: a false positive restarts a healthy daemon.
   300	STALL_EXIT_SECONDS = float(os.environ.get("XCODE_MCP_FRONT_STALL_EXIT", "180"))
   301	
   302	# How often to look for the approval dialog while a call is blocked waiting for it. Short,
   303	# because the window is the length of one blocked list_tools and a missed dialog costs the
   304	# whole connection attempt.
   305	ALLOW_CLICK_POLL_SECONDS = float(os.environ.get("XCODE_MCP_FRONT_CLICK_POLL", "1.5"))
   306	
   307	# Set the first time we see a foreign dialog while we are not connected; cleared whenever we
   308	# see no dialog or our own. Module-level rather than per-upstream because Xcode shows one
   309	# dialog at a time for the whole application, so this is genuinely global state.
   310	_foreign_dialog_first_seen: dict = {}
   311	
   312	
   313	async def _click_allow_if_present() -> bool:
   314	    """Best-effort, narrowly scoped: only ever touches a button whose title is
   315	    the exact literal string "Allow" or "Don't Allow", only in Xcode's own
   316	    process. This is the one dialog this tool exists to eat, not a general
   317	    click-any-prompt macro. Harmless no-op for an upstream (like
   318	    drews-xcode-mcp) that never triggers this dialog at all — it just never
   319	    finds anything to click.
   320	
   321	    Xcode's approval dialogs do NOT stack (confirmed live, 2026-08-14 — only
   322	    one is ever showing; a new connection attempt's own dialog stays hidden
   323	    behind whatever's currently up until that one is dismissed). So a single
   324	    stale, never-answered dialog can permanently block every future connection
   325	    attempt, including this daemon's own, unless something clears it.
   326	
   327	    The decision, once a dialog's own PID is read from its text:
   328	      - matches OUR current pid            -> click Allow
   329	      - doesn't match, and that pid is DEAD -> click Don't Allow (nobody's
   330	        waiting on it — always safe, regardless of whose it originally was;
   331	        no history-tracking needed, this can't deadlock behind a dialog it
   332	        doesn't "remember")
   333	      - doesn't match, and that pid is ALIVE -> leave it alone; it's someone
   334	        else's legitimate in-flight request, not ours to approve OR deny
   335	    """
   336	    my_pid = str(os.getpid())
   337	    # Iterate every window, not just window 1 — found by convocation review,
   338	    # 2026-08-14: the approval dialog isn't guaranteed to be window 1 (e.g. a
   339	    # project window frontmost, or the dialog opening as window 2). Window 1
   340	    # only would silently never find the dialog and leave it unclicked
   341	    # forever. check-allow-window.sh already iterated all windows correctly;
   342	    # this production path didn't match it.
   343	    read_script = """
   344	tell application "System Events" to tell process "Xcode"
   345	  repeat with w in windows
   346	    if exists (button "Allow" of w) then
   347	      set winText to ""
   348	      try
   349	        set winText to (value of every static text of w) as string
   350	      end try
   351	      return winText
   352	    end if
   353	  end repeat
   354	  return ""
   355	end tell
   356	"""
   357	    try:
   358	        out = await _run_osascript(read_script)
   359	    except Exception as e:
   360	        log.warning("checking for an approval dialog failed (will retry, but if this repeats, check "
   361	                     "whether the wrapper .app's Accessibility/Automation grant is still valid): %s", e)
   362	        return False
   363	
   364	    text = out.decode(errors="replace")
   365	    if "PID: " not in text:
   366	        return False  # no dialog showing — the normal case most ticks
   367	
   368	    dialog_pid = text.split("PID: ", 1)[1].split()[0].strip()
   369	
   370	    if dialog_pid == my_pid:
   371	        _foreign_dialog_first_seen.pop("pid", None)
   372	        action = "Allow"
   373	    else:
   374	        # A GRACE PERIOD, BECAUSE CLEARING UNCONDITIONALLY MONOPOLISES XCODE. The previous
   375	        # version denied every foreign PID on sight. That unblocks us and starves everyone
   376	        # else: a legitimate agent's dialog gets Don't Allow, it retries, and it lands in the
   377	        # same denial loop forever, so this daemon owns Xcode and nothing else can use it.
   378	        # GhOST-OpenClaw caught it in peer review of the commit that introduced it.
   379	        #
   380	        # The bounded version keeps both properties. Somebody else's prompt is left alone
   381	        # while there is any chance they are about to answer it — which is the normal case,
   382	        # since a live agent's own approval usually resolves in seconds. Only once the SAME
   383	        # foreign dialog has been sitting there long enough to have stopped being anyone's
   384	        # in-flight request do we clear it so ours can surface. Nothing is destroyed either
   385	        # way: a denied agent re-prompts on its next attempt.
   386	        first = _foreign_dialog_first_seen.get("pid")
   387	        now = time.monotonic()
   388	        if first is None or first[0] != dialog_pid:
   389	            _foreign_dialog_first_seen["pid"] = (dialog_pid, now)
   390	            log.debug("a foreign approval dialog (pid %s) is in the way; leaving it for %.0fs "
   391	                      "in case its own agent answers it", dialog_pid, FOREIGN_DIALOG_GRACE_SECONDS)
   392	            return False
   393	        if now - first[1] < FOREIGN_DIALOG_GRACE_SECONDS:
   394	            return False
   395	        log.info("foreign approval dialog for pid %s has blocked us for %.0fs — clearing it so "
   396	                 "our own prompt can surface; that process re-prompts on its next attempt",
   397	                 dialog_pid, now - first[1])
   398	        # ANYTHING ELSE IN FRONT OF OURS GETS CLEARED, live or dead. This used to leave a
   399	        # LIVE unfamiliar PID's dialog strictly alone, out of politeness to another agent's
   400	        # in-flight request. That politeness is unaffordable here: Xcode's dialogs do not
   401	        # stack, so a single stranger's unanswered prompt sits in front of ours forever and
   402	        # the daemon can never connect again — which is exactly what a leftover
   403	        # "Allow 'Codex' to access Xcode?" from a convocation run did on 2026-08-30, and
   404	        # what the two front daemons did to each other for the sixteen days before that.
   405	        #
   406	        # Denying is not destructive and that is what makes this safe. A denied agent is
   407	        # told no for one connection attempt and re-prompts on its next one, by which time
   408	        # ours is out of the way. Jonathan, 2026-08-30: "I don't see why this needs
   409	        # exclusive access. Just run it saying with PID you care about or whatever?"
   410	        #
   411	        # The sibling branch above still matters and is not redundant: two front daemons
   412	        # denying each other would ping-pong, each clearing the other's prompt on every
   413	        # retry. Family gets Allow, everyone else gets out of the way.
   414	        action = "Don’t Allow"
   415	    click_script = f"""
   416	tell application "System Events" to tell process "Xcode"
   417	  repeat with w in windows
   418	    if exists (button "{action}" of w) then
   419	      click (button "{action}" of w)
   420	      return "clicked"

